// --- CONFIGURAZIONE ---
B := int(1,6);   // B is DISCRETE: the slider snaps to the integer on release

// How many of the 6 declared tubes are actually on screen. The "Mesh" selector of
// the renderer dock stops at this number: without it, it would always reach 6 and you
// could select a tube that is not drawn, moving its sliders for nothing.
// It is NOT simply B: the cutout limits the count to Nmaxc, which drops from
// 6 to 3 when the tubes DEGENERATE (A at 45 degrees, |cos(2Rc)| < 0.02), where the six
// branches collapse into three. The same rule is repeated here with the syntax of the
// CPU evaluator: if(cond, then, else) instead of step/mix.
MESH_VISIBLE := min(max(floor(min(max(B,1),6)+0.5),1), if(abs(cos(2*(min(max(A,0.3),10)*0.078539816)))<0.02, 3, 6));
u_min := 0;
u_max := 6.28318531;
v_min := 0;
v_max := 6.28318531;    // multi-mesh: each tube has its own grid
steps := 600;           // the seams no longer exist: no divisibility constraint
A := min(0.3);   // below 0.3 the tubes collapse onto the axis: only artifacts

// --- MULTI-MESH: one tube per grid ---
// The six meshes are declared STATICALLY (they are fixed at Run), while
// slider B picks live how many tubes to show: the meshes with index >= N
// are discarded ENTIRELY by the cutout, so B stays continuous and the
// geometry does not need to be rebuilt. `mesh` = 0..5 identifies the tube
// (C12, C13, C14, C23, C24, C34).
// Before multi-mesh the six tubes were stitched into ONE grid with v over
// [0,12*PI], compressed onto the N actual branches: they needed the scale vs, the index
// kk by division, the rescale f01 and FIVE guard bands G1..G5 in the
// cutout (which left as many circular grooves). All gone: no
// quad can connect two separate meshes.
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 100
v: 0, 6.28318531, 100
//MESH_END
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 100
v: 0, 6.28318531, 100
//MESH_END
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 100
v: 0, 6.28318531, 100
//MESH_END
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 100
v: 0, 6.28318531, 100
//MESH_END
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 100
v: 0, 6.28318531, 100
//MESH_END
//MESH_BEGIN
u: 0, 6.28318531, 100
v: 0, 6.28318531, 100
//MESH_END

// --- THE CLIFFORD TORI IN S^3: THE SKELETON OF THE 16-CELL ---
// NB: do not declare variables named PI, pi, tau: the translator
// replaces them with the numeric value throughout the script.
//
// At a radius r < PI/4 the surfaces x_i^2 + x_j^2 = cos^2 r are SIX, not
// three: one for each pair of indices. They are the tubes around the six great
// coordinate circles
//     C12  C13  C14  C23  C24  C34
// which meet three at a time at the eight points +-e1, +-e2, +-e3, +-e4:
// e1 lies on C12, C13, C14, and so on. Those eight points are the vertices and the
// twenty-four arcs are the edges of the 16-cell, the 4-dimensional polytope
// dual to the hypercube: this figure is its thickened skeleton.
// Two complementary axes (C12 and C34) never touch: they are 90 degrees apart
// everywhere, so their tubes do not interpenetrate.
//
// --- OPENING ON SLIDER A ---
// A is the angular radius of the tubes in steps of 4.5 degrees, i.e. PI/40, so
// the slider's full scale coincides with the limit of the construction:
//     A =  2.67 -> 12 degrees (the historical value)
//     A =  5    -> PI/8 = 22.5 degrees
//     A = 10    -> PI/4 = 45 degrees, where each tube becomes half a hypersphere and
//                  each surface coincides with that of the complementary axis.
// One notch of the slider is 0.045 degrees.
// Below the radius at which the path moves away from the axis at the joints (6.3 degrees
// on the three- and six-tube paths, 2.5 on the double tube) the camera leaves the tubes
// on the curves.
// It is on A because F is left to the textures, which use it as a slider (e.g.
// Static Holography: density of the grid): on the same slider,
// dragging the radius would change the pattern too. The radius enters only the position
// of the vertices and the cutting threshold, never the seam machinery,
// so it moves live without rebuilding the mesh.
//
// --- NUMBER OF TUBES ON SLIDER B ---
// B picks how many of the six tubes to draw, from 1 to 6, in the order
//     C12  C13  C14  C23  C24  C34
// Unlike the radius (slider A), this enters the seam machinery: changing it
// REBUILDS the mesh, so the slider snaps instead of sliding. The domain v
// stays fixed at 12*PI and the requested branches are compressed into it: this is
// why steps must stay divisible by 1..6 (600 is), so that
// every seam falls on an exact vertex even in float32.
//
// Near PI/4 the number is truncated to THREE by itself. There
// cos^2 R = sin^2 R = 1/2, so the tube around Cij and the one around
// the complementary axis Ckl are the SAME surface: the pairs are
// C12<->C34, C13<->C24, C14<->C23. Drawing more than three would mean
// drawing the same surface twice, with z-fighting. The first three branches
// of the order above contain no complementary pairs, so the
// truncation already gives three distinct surfaces without reordering anything.
// The distance between the two complementary surfaces is |cos 2R|: it is 1 at zero
// radius and goes to zero continuously at PI/4. It is truncated when it drops below
// 0.02 (from about 44.4 degrees up): there the two surfaces are already so
// close that they z-fight, well before coinciding exactly. Measuring the
// separation instead of the angle keeps the jump from depending on the last
// digit of the radius.
// Below A = 0.3 (1.35 degrees) the tubes collapse onto their own axis: the
// surface degenerates into the great circle and only visual artifacts remain
// (interpenetrating faces, unstable normals). The minimum of the
// clamp is NOT 0: it is the smallest radius that still draws a tube.
float Rq = clamp(A, 0.3, 10.0) * 0.078539816;
float degenere = 1.0 - step(0.02, abs(cos(2.0 * Rq)));
float Nmax = mix(6.0, 3.0, degenere);
float N = clamp(floor(clamp(B, 1.0, 6.0) + 0.5), 1.0, Nmax);

float R = Rq;
float cR = cos(R);
float sR = sin(R);


// The branch index is the mesh index: no divisions or epsilons to
// decide which side of a seam a vertex falls on (it was the most
// fragile point between Mali and Adreno).
float kk = mesh;


// Each branch puts cos R on the plane of its own axis and sin R on the two remaining
// indices: (a,b) turn along the axis, (c,d) around the tube.
float a = cR * cos(u);
float b = cR * sin(u);
float c = sR * cos(v);
float d = sR * sin(v);

// Branch indicators: kk is an exact integer (a sum of steps), so
// max(0, 1 - |kk - n|) is 1 on branch n and 0 on all the others. With six
// branches this form stays readable where six nested mix() would not.
float I0 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(kk));         // C12
float I1 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(kk - 1.0));   // C13
float I2 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(kk - 2.0));   // C14
float I3 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(kk - 3.0));   // C23
float I4 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(kk - 4.0));   // C24
float I5 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(kk - 5.0));   // C34

// NB: the validator in inputvalidator.cpp requires the literal form of the
// return with vec3 or vec4 opened right after it, and counts the parentheses over the
// whole text, COMMENTS INCLUDED: do not write unbalanced ones here.
return vec4(
    a * (I0 + I1 + I2) + c * (I3 + I4 + I5),
    b * I0 + c * (I1 + I2) + a * (I3 + I4) + d * I5,
    c * I0 + b * (I1 + I3) + d * (I2 + I4) + a * I5,
    d * (I0 + I1 + I3) + b * (I2 + I4 + I5)
);

//CUTOUT_BEGIN
// --- CUTTING ONLY THE INNER OBSTRUCTIONS ---
// From each tube we remove the part that lies inside the OTHER DRAWN TUBES:
// what remains is the boundary of the union of the solids, i.e. a CLOSED
// surface. Each tube is cut open around the vertices it touches, and there
// the passages toward the other tubes open up.
// Exact criterion: a point lies inside the tube around Cij if its
// angular distance from that circle is < R, i.e. if x_i^2 + x_j^2 > cos^2 R.
// On its own branch its own quantity is identically cos^2 R, so it is
// zeroed with (1 - indicator) and the maximum of the others is taken.
// The tubes NOT drawn must be excluded from the maximum with the same weight: at N < 6
// a missing tube must cut nothing, otherwise holes would open
// toward a surface that is not there.
// same radius as the body, from the same slider: if the two copies diverged
// the cut would no longer fall on the surface it is trimming
float Rc = clamp(A, 0.3, 10.0) * 0.078539816;
float cRc = cos(Rc);
float sRc = sin(Rc);
float lim = cRc * cRc;

// same threshold as the body: if the two copies diverged, the cut would use a
// number of tubes different from the one drawn
float degc = 1.0 - step(0.02, abs(cos(2.0 * Rc)));
float Nmaxc = mix(6.0, 3.0, degc);
float Nc = clamp(floor(clamp(B, 1.0, 6.0) + 0.5), 1.0, Nmaxc);


// branch index = mesh index, as in the body
float tk = mesh;


float aa = cRc * cos(u);
float bb = cRc * sin(u);
float cc = sRc * cos(v);
float dd = sRc * sin(v);

float J0 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(tk));
float J1 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(tk - 1.0));
float J2 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(tk - 2.0));
float J3 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(tk - 3.0));
float J4 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(tk - 4.0));
float J5 = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(tk - 5.0));

// Presence of the six tubes: P_n is 1 if branch n is drawn (n < Nc).
float P0 = step(0.5, Nc);
float P1 = step(1.5, Nc);
float P2 = step(2.5, Nc);
float P3 = step(3.5, Nc);
float P4 = step(4.5, Nc);
float P5 = step(5.5, Nc);

// rebuilds the point of THIS fragment, as in the body
float px = aa * (J0 + J1 + J2) + cc * (J3 + J4 + J5);
float py = bb * J0 + cc * (J1 + J2) + aa * (J3 + J4) + dd * J5;
float pz = cc * J0 + bb * (J1 + J3) + dd * (J2 + J4) + aa * J5;
float pw = dd * (J0 + J1 + J3) + bb * (J2 + J4 + J5);

float q12 = px * px + py * py;
float q13 = px * px + pz * pz;
float q14 = px * px + pw * pw;
float q23 = py * py + pz * pz;
float q24 = py * py + pw * pw;
float q34 = pz * pz + pw * pw;

// (1 - J) zeroes the tube's OWN term, P zeroes the tubes not drawn.
float m1 = max(q12 * (1.0 - J0) * P0, q13 * (1.0 - J1) * P1);
float m2 = max(q14 * (1.0 - J2) * P2, q23 * (1.0 - J3) * P3);
float m3 = max(q24 * (1.0 - J4) * P4, q34 * (1.0 - J5) * P5);
float altro = max(max(m1, m2), m3);

// The 0.0001 margin keeps the points lying EXACTLY on the intersection
// curves, where the quantity is exactly cos^2 R: they are the edges along
// which the pieces weld together, and in float32 they must be kept.
bool didCut = (altro > lim + 0.0001);

// --- MESHES BEYOND N: discarded entirely ---
// The six meshes always exist, but slider B (and the clamp to 3 when the radius
// degenerates) decides how many are active: the others are discarded here. This is what
// keeps B continuous without rebuilding the geometry.
// Gone instead are the FIVE guard bands G1..G5 on the seams, the
// rescale f01 and the step computation: there is no longer any joining quad
// to mask.
if (mesh > Nc - 0.5) { didCut = true; }

return didCut;
//CUTOUT_END
